---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="editor">
  <h2 id="live">Live Markdown</h2>
  <p>
    HelloNotes styles Markdown as you type. Headings grow, emphasis takes effect, lists and task
    lists gain bullets and checkboxes, tables align — all while the file on disk stays exactly the
    Markdown you typed. Nothing is rewritten behind your back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move the caret onto a line and that line’s raw syntax is revealed so you can edit it; move away
    and it tidies itself again. Reveal is per line, so the rest of a multi-line blockquote or list
    keeps its formatting while you work inside it. This is the “caret-driven concealment” you may
    know from Obsidian or Bear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Formatting commands are in the <strong>Format</strong> menu, with the shortcuts you would
    expect — see <a href={href('/manual/shortcuts')}>Keyboard shortcuts</a>. They act in Edit; in
    Preview, Markdown and Split they are dimmed. On iPad they are also
    on the row iPadOS floats over the keyboard, and on iPhone on a bar of their own directly above
    the keys.
  </p>

  <h2 id="modes">View modes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Mode</th><th>What it shows</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Edit</strong></td><td>The live editor. This is where you write.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Preview</strong></td><td>Read-only, rendered exactly as GitHub would render it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Markdown</strong></td><td>The raw source, unstyled.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Split</strong></td><td>Source and rendered page side by side — or one above the other, where the pane is taller than it is wide.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The four buttons at the bottom of the note switch between them, as do <kbd>⌘1</kbd> to{' '}
    <kbd>⌘4</kbd> and the <strong>View</strong> menu.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Preview is rendered with <strong>cmark-gfm</strong>, the same engine GitHub uses, so a note
    that looks right here looks right in a README.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lists">Lists, blank lines and room to write</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Return continues a list.</strong> The next bullet appears — the next number in a
      numbered list, and an empty box after a task. It works in Edit and Markdown alike, inside a
      quote too, and never in code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Return on an empty item ends the list.</strong> A nested item moves out a level; one
      at the list’s own level leaves a blank line with the caret below it, ready for a paragraph.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Tasks</strong> can be ticked in Edit.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every blank line you type shows in Edit.</strong> One between two paragraphs is the
      usual gap, and each one after it is an empty line. Preview closes a run of them up to that
      one gap, as GitHub does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A long note scrolls on past its end</strong>, in every view, until its last line is
      about halfway up — so the line you are writing need not sit at the bottom edge of the window.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="column">The text column</h2>
  <p>
    How wide the text runs is a setting, and it applies in every view — Edit, Preview and Markdown
    lay the note out in the same column, so switching between them does not move the text
    sideways. The controls are in <strong>Settings ▸ Appearance</strong>, under{' '}
    <strong>Text width</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Reading width</strong> — the longest a line of text gets: Narrow (60 characters),
      Normal (80), Wide (90), or Full width. A column held to one of these is centred in the pane.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Editor width</strong> — how much of the pane you write in: Full pane, 90%, 75% or
      50%, left-aligned.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrap guide</strong> — a line drawn at 72, 80 or 100 characters while you edit. It is
      a guide you can see, not a wrap point.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Whatever the column, the note fills its pane: the scroll bar stays at the pane’s edge, and
    scrolling over the margin scrolls the note.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rich">Code, maths and diagrams</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Code blocks</strong> are syntax-highlighted by language, using the GitHub theme.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>LaTeX maths</strong> renders natively — <code>$inline$</code> and <code>$$display$$</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mermaid diagrams</strong> in a <code>```mermaid</code> block are drawn inline, natively.
      The <strong>View diagram</strong> button in a diagram’s corner shows it larger, with zoom; in
      Edit, clicking the diagram itself opens its source.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In Edit none of these is a web view, so they scroll and type as smoothly as plain text.
    Preview — and the page half of Split — is one, rendering through the same engine GitHub uses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="callouts">Callouts, comments and properties</h2>
  <p>
    Obsidian-style callouts work as you would expect, with icons and collapsible bodies:
  </p>
  <pre
    class="my-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-card border border-line bg-card p-4 text-[13px] text-body"><code>&gt; [!note] Worth remembering
&gt; The body of the callout goes here.</code></pre>
  <p>
    Text wrapped in <code>%%double percent signs%%</code> is dimmed as a comment. YAML front matter
    at the top of a note is presented in the right panel’s <strong>Properties</strong> view as
    typed, editable fields — text, number, checkbox, date and list — rather than raw YAML.
  </p>

  <h2 id="finding">Finding and replacing</h2>
  <p>
    <kbd>⌘F</kbd> — or <strong>Find &amp; Replace in Note</strong>, under the magnifying glass in
    the bar — opens the find bar over the current note, with <strong>Replace</strong> and{' '}
    <strong>Replace All</strong>. <kbd>Return</kbd> goes to the next match, the arrows step back and
    forward, and <strong>Done</strong> or <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes it. Finding works in the Edit view,
    so from another view <kbd>⌘F</kbd> switches to Edit first.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong><code>.*</code></strong> button makes the search a <strong>regular
    expression</strong>, and stays on until you turn it off:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>\d+</code> finds numbers; <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> match at the start and end
      of every line.
    </li>
    <li>
      A pattern ignores case, as a plain search does, unless it starts with <code>(?-i)</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the replacement, <code>$1</code> is the first group in brackets (<code>$0</code> is the
      whole match), and <code>\n</code> and <code>\t</code> are a new line and a tab. Finding{' '}
      <code>(\w+)@(\w+)</code> and replacing with <code>$2 for $1</code> turns <em>me@home</em> into{' '}
      <em>home for me</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A pattern that cannot be read shows <strong>Invalid pattern</strong> where the count goes,
      and one that runs for more than half a second shows <strong>Too slow</strong> and stops
      rather than holding up your typing.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To search across every open collection instead, choose <strong>Search Notes</strong> under the
    same magnifying glass, or press <kbd>⌥⌘F</kbd> — see{' '}
    <a href={href('/manual/organising')}>Organising notes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="images">Images and attachments</h2>
  <p>
    Paste an image and it is written into your collection as a PNG with a relative Markdown link —
    by default into an <code>assets</code> folder beside the note, which you can change in{' '}
    <strong>Settings ▸ General</strong>. Non-Markdown files in the folder (PDFs, images, CSVs)
    appear in the tree when <strong>View ▸ Show Non-Note Files</strong> is on, and open in a native
    viewer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pasting rich text from a browser converts it to clean Markdown rather than pasting HTML.
  </p>

  <h2 id="windows">Tabs, windows and export</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Notes open in tabs in the bar over the editor.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Open in New Window</strong> — in the <strong>Note</strong> menu and in{' '}
      <strong>Note Actions</strong> — puts a note in its own window for side-by-side work.
    </li>
    <li><strong>File ▸ Export as HTML…</strong> and <strong>File ▸ Export as PDF…</strong> write the current note out.</li>
    <li>
      A note with Marp front matter gains <strong>Present as Slides</strong> in{' '}
      <strong>Note Actions</strong>: a slide-deck preview.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="stats">Statistics and outline</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Summary &amp; Outline</strong>, the first view of the right panel, shows the note’s
    word, character and paragraph counts and its reading time, and lists its headings — click one
    to jump straight to that section.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
